tect her  identity she grew up able to go to  school worked as a photographer and  traveled  not anymore the only job that i have is  sitting at home and crying for  all the things which i lost not only me  all afghan people this is new  afghanistan barack said says the country  the taliban now controls has changed a  new generation grew up knowing freedoms  and going to school  the protests in the early weeks after  the taliban returned showed the  difference  they want  peace  they want  dignity they want to be counted as a  human barak zai says technology is  making it harder for the taliban to keep  its brutality from getting out  she also has a theory that where there  is oppression and pressure there is  resistance and that with this new  generation  that resistance  will build like armstrong global news  and on the new reality mike takes a  deeper look at what the loss of  education for women in afghanistan will  mean for future generations that's  tomorrow at 7 here on global  thanking the front lines up next the  treats and tributes for canada's health  workers  [Applause]  welcome back here  canada's governor general is starting  off her thanksgiving weekend with a  visit to the ottawa mission mary mae  simon helping to hand out food and hot  meals to the city's most vulnerable the  governor general also took the time to  meet some of the missions clients and  learn more about how they shelter and  support the capital's homeless  population  well we started the pandemic banging  pots and pans in honor of health care  workers now they're more likely to face  abuse from protesters defying public  health measures but as heather urex west  tells us there are still some small acts  of kindness that mean a lot to those on  the front lines of the copic fight  four weeks ago elizabeth prost buried  her dad he didn't die of covet 19 but on  the day of the funeral a coveted  exposure put her mom's care facility  into lockdown robbing her of the chance  to say goodbye i was really really angry  driving back to calgary from  saskatchewan  and i thought well  that's  something  and i thought  what would my dad do what would my dad  do it's amazing thank you so much  instead of being angry she decided she'd  be kind  organizing a morning of treats for  health care workers outside this calgary  hospital people want to do something  they want to express their appreciation  they want to express their love their  gratitude when alberta coveted  restrictions canceled the private events  for calgary's devour catering the  company began selling care packages for  those working in the icu  within days 6 500 meals had been sold a  thousand will be delivered thanksgiving  day the response has been  awe inspiring like i'm i  i am amazed  other gestures have been smaller  colorful signs and costumes to welcome  doctors and nurses coming on shift  a healing jingle dance for those  suffering in the hospital with covert  19. it's a blessing  to everyone who's around us they feel  they feel that power  we ha we try to help uplift the people  who are here or handmade cards created  to spark a smile i just wanted to write  something from the heart  something nice and warming to give the  health  frontline workers a boost of energy when  they read the card for those on the  front lines these kind acts have lifted  spirits it's really nice to see that  despite  um  despite feeling like nobody's listening  clearly there are some people listening  and hearing what we have to say and and  appreciating the work that we're doing  and so it feels really nice with the  fourth wave crisis far from done it's a  bit of fuel for the soul to help health  care workers carry on  heather urex west global news calgary  and that is global national for this  friday i'm sophie louie tonight's your  canada is a cinnabon forest in winnipeg  thanks for watching far nasser will be  here at the anchor desk tomorrow have a  happy thanksgiving  [Music]
 

